NAME
       roffsub - check, compile, and render terminal manuals written in roffsub

SYNOPSIS
       roffsub [-w width] [-s style] [-m measure] [file]
       roffsub -e [-s style] [file]
       roffsub -c file ...
       roffsub -i [-d date] [-r rev] [file]
       roffsub -p [-w width] [file]
       roffsub -g c|bash name [file]
       roffsub -n [-d date] [-r rev] [file]
       roffsub -h | --help | -V

DESCRIPTION
       roffsub works with manuals written in roffsub, a subset of the man macros and tbl that every roff program
       displays and that small awk and C routines can also render. It renders a manual for the terminal in use, at that
       terminal's width, with bold and italic where the terminal can show them. It also checks a manual's form, compiles
       it into records, formats it at a fixed width, and writes the text as a C array or a bash heredoc for a program to
       carry.

       roffsub comes as a C binary and as a bash function that behave alike. The function serves systems where programs
       cannot be run from the user's own directories, such as a home directory mounted noexec, so one package works
       everywhere. Both use awk to check, compile, and generate; the binary renders records and pre-formatted text
       without it.

OPTIONS
       -w width
              Render at width columns. Without it the width comes from MANWIDTH, then from the terminal, then 80; output
              that is not a terminal is 80 columns unless MANWIDTH is set.

       -s style
              Write emphasis as plain (none), overstrike (backspace sequences for a pager), sgr (terminal control
              sequences), or pre (font escapes for later emission). Without it the style follows the destination; see
              OUTPUT CHOICES.

       -m measure
              Never fill text past measure columns, however wide the terminal.

       -e     Emit pre-formatted text, as written by -p, in the chosen style.

       -c     Check the form of each file and report every departure from roffsub as file:line: id: message. The exit
              status is 1 when an error was found. With ROFFSUB_STRICT set, warnings count as errors too.

       -i     Write the compiled records of file. -d and -r fill in @DATE@ and @VERSION@ on the .TH line.

       -p     Write file formatted at a fixed width (default 80) with its font changes kept, for a program's
              preformatted profile.

       -g c|bash name
              Write the lines of file as a C array called name, or as a marked bash region that reads them into the
              variable name.

       -n     Write file as the page to install for man. -d and -r fill in the .TH line as for -i, and each table is
              rewritten so that roff draws it as roffsub does: no vertical lines, and a + where a rule meets a column
              gap. The source itself stays as written.

       -h     Print the usage summary.

       --help Show this manual.

       -V     Print the version.

       A file may be a roffsub source or its compiled records; records begin with the line v, a tab, and 1. Without a
       file the standard input is read.

OUTPUT CHOICES
        OUTPUT                   GOES TO            STYLE        WIDTH
       ------------------------+------------------+------------+----------
        terminal                 the pager          overstrike   terminal
        terminal, PAGER is cat   the terminal       sgr          terminal
        file or pipe             the file or pipe   plain        80

       The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less when installed, otherwise more. TERM=dumb selects plain. Command-line
       options override the environment, and the environment overrides these defaults.

ENVIRONMENT
       MANWIDTH
              The rendering width.

       COLUMNS
              The terminal width, when MANWIDTH is not set.

       PAGER  The pager for terminal output.

       ROFFSUB_STYLE
              The style: plain, overstrike, sgr, or pre.

       ROFFSUB_SGR
              Control sequence parameters for bold and italic, as b=1;33:i=4;36. The defaults are 1 (bold) and 4
              (underline). Colors let bold and italic show as distinct colors.

       ROFFSUB_MEASURE
              The maximum line length.

       ROFFSUB_HEADING
              The number of columns section headings are moved in from the margin; 0 by default. Everything else stays
              in place.

       ROFFSUB_STRICT
              When set, the check treats warnings as errors.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success, 1 when a check found errors, 2 on a usage or input error.

EXAMPLES
       Check a manual, then read it as a program would show it:

       roffsub -c srt.1
       roffsub srt.1
       roffsub -w 50 -s plain srt.1 > srt.txt

       Embed the full manual in a C program and in a bash function:

       roffsub -i -d 2026-11-15 -r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.ir
       roffsub -g c srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.h
       roffsub -g bash _srt_man srt.ir > srt.man.bash

       Write the page to install:

       roffsub -n -d 2026-11-15 -r r0.9 srt.1 > srt.1.out

       Embed a pre-formatted manual in a small program:

       roffsub -p srt.1 > srt.pre
       roffsub -g c srt_man srt.pre > srt.man.h

FILES
        roffsub-check.awk    the form check
        roffsub-ir.awk       the compiler, source to records
        roffsub-render.awk   the renderer, records to terminal text
        roffsub-emit.awk     the emitter, pre-formatted text to terminal text
        roffsub-gen.awk      embedding as a C array or bash region
        roffsub-man.awk      the page to install for man
        roffsub.h            the renderer in C
        roffsub-emit.h       the emitter in C
        roffsub-tty.h        width, style, and pager choice in C

SEE ALSO
       roffsub(7), man(1), less(1)
